  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 249 PM EDT WED SEP 23 2026\n\nOverall, the long term period will be characterized by cool and \nquiet weather, owing to a fairly persistent midlevel ridge west of\nthe forecast area. At the start of the period, a vertically \nstacked low will be located off of the North Carolina coast, with \na highly amplified ridge over the central CONUS. This low will \ndeepen as it lifts into the Northeast, blocking any eastward \nprogression of the ridge. Eastern Kentucky will find itself on the\nwestern edge of northeastern troughing through at least the \nweekend, with most guidance suggesting that this system will lift \nfar north into Canada by early next week. This will allow the \nridge axis to progress eastward, with the next major system not \napproaching until the very end of the forecast period.\n\nAt the surface, this large scale pattern should keep weather cool\nand dry through the weekend. Largely northeasterly winds around a\nsurface high over the Great Lakes will relegate dewpoints to the \n40s, peaking in the mid 50s at their wettest. Even with mostly \nclear skies each day, highs will range from the upper 60s to mid \n70s. In short, it will finally feel less like summer and more like\nfall. Overnight, clear skies and light winds will enable \nefficient radiational cooling, with ridge valley splits expected \nto develop. MOS guidance suggests temperatures will bottom out in \nthe low 40s in particularly sheltered valley locations on \nSaturday, Sunday, and Monday mornings. Given that most guidance \nagrees temperatures could fall into the mid 40s overnight (even \nwithout considering terrain factors), this seems very feasible. It\nmay be time to get your jacket out.\n\nBy early next week, temperatures will warm somewhat as the ridge \nexpands eastward. Highs will climb into the low 80s in many \nlocations by Tuesday, although weather will still be dry. As of \nnow, the next rain chance looks to be Wednesday as a large scale \nsystem approaches, but the details remain fuzzy this far out.",
